From: ePass maintainers <epass@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] pcm: preserve the RW application pointer in state queries

On architectures without status/control mmap, pcm_state() uses SYNC_PTR.
Passing zero flags writes the cached application pointer back to the
kernel. RW transfers advance only the kernel pointer, so the state query
before the next transfer rewinds it and can prevent playback from ever
reaching its start threshold.

Read the current kernel control values for RW streams. Keep the existing
pointer commit behavior for MMAP streams.

Upstream: Not submitted
Signed-off-by: ePass maintainers <epass@localhost>
---
 src/pcm.c | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/pcm.c b/src/pcm.c
--- a/src/pcm.c
+++ b/src/pcm.c
@@ -1404,7 +1404,10 @@
 
 int pcm_state(struct pcm *pcm)
 {
-    int err = pcm_sync_ptr(pcm, 0);
+    /* RW ioctls advance the kernel appl_ptr, not our fallback copy. */
+    int flags = (pcm->flags & PCM_MMAP) ? 0 :
+        SNDRV_PCM_SYNC_PTR_APPL | SNDRV_PCM_SYNC_PTR_AVAIL_MIN;
+    int err = pcm_sync_ptr(pcm, flags);
     if (err < 0)
         return err;
 
